---
description:
globs:
alwaysApply: true
---

- Whenever I ask you to solve an issue for me, please list out all the most likely reasons why you think this issue
  could occur. Then tell me what you think the most likely solutions are. Then tell me what your preferred solution is.
  Don't implement a solution for the problem until I explicitly tell you to proceed with implementation (this signal
  must come after you you research the issue(s) and diagnose the potential solution(s)).
- After implementing or modifying any code, always verify the changes by running `make lint test` from the repository
  root. This ensures that linting passes and all tests succeed before completing the task.
- Always write out types and type hints for every variable, parameter, struct, field, return type, etc.
- Make sure to always implement code with security in mind. If we develop something insecurely/unsafely, then please
  bring it to my attention. Mention the possible issue(s) and their potential solution(s). Don't code immediately - just
  discuss with me, then I'll tell you to proceed with an implementation or not.
- By default, make sure to follow all standard coding practices for the given languages we build with in this repo:
  Rust, Typescript. But make sure to personalize the code to follow existing code patterns and error handling.
- If Rust/Clippy says to use `#[allow]`, use `#[expect]` instead.
- When writing unit tests, always assert an "expected" value against an "actual" value. If asserting objects, try to
  compare 100% of all fields/sub-fields if possible (e.g. deterministic). Sometimes asserting 100% of all fields is not
  possible, like if returned data is a generated timestamp, UUID, etc.
